/**
 * Book 5 Generator: Part ONE - Understanding the Literature Review (Ch 1-4)
 * and Part TWO - Planning and Searching the Literature (Ch 5-8)
 * NO EM DASHES anywhere.
 */

function ch01() {
  return `<h1>Chapter 1</h1><h2 style='font-size:2rem;margin-bottom:4px;'>What a Literature Review Is and What It Is Not</h2><blockquote><strong>Learning Objectives</strong><br>By the end of this chapter, you will be able to:<br>* Define a literature review and explain its purpose in academic research<br>* Distinguish a literature review from an annotated bibliography, a book report, and a research essay<br>* Explain why the literature review is an argument, not a summary<br>* Identify the key functions a literature review serves in a thesis, dissertation, or journal article<br>* Recognise common misconceptions about the literature review and correct them</blockquote><h2>1.1 Defining the Literature Review</h2>
<p>A literature review is a critical, analytical account of the existing research on a particular topic. It identifies, evaluates, and synthesises the relevant scholarly work that has been produced on a specific research question or area of inquiry. The literature review is one of the most important components of any academic research project, yet it is also one of the most frequently misunderstood. Many students approach it as a summary of everything they have read, when in reality it is a carefully constructed argument that demonstrates the researcher's command of the field and justifies the need for their proposed study (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p>The word "review" is misleading. In everyday language, a review implies a description or evaluation of a single item, such as a book review or a film review. An academic literature review is fundamentally different. It is not a review of a single source but a synthesis of an entire body of scholarship. The researcher must read dozens, sometimes hundreds, of sources, extract the key findings and arguments from each, identify patterns and contradictions across the body of work, and weave these elements together into a coherent narrative that serves a specific scholarly purpose (Ridley, 2012).</p>
<p>At its core, a literature review serves three fundamental purposes. First, it demonstrates that the researcher is familiar with the existing knowledge in their field. No serious scholar can claim to contribute new knowledge without first understanding what is already known. Second, it provides the theoretical and empirical foundation for the proposed study. The literature review establishes the concepts, frameworks, and findings upon which the new research will build. Third, and most importantly, it identifies the gap in the existing knowledge that the proposed study will address. Without this gap, there is no justification for conducting the research (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>

<div style="margin:22px 0;text-align:center;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 540 300" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto;"><text x="270" y="28" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1a2332" font-size="14" font-weight="700" font-family="Inter,sans-serif">THE THREE PURPOSES OF A LITERATURE REVIEW</text><rect x="20" y="55" width="160" height="120" rx="8" fill="#1a3a5c"/><text x="100" y="78" text-anchor="middle" fill="#c9a227" font-size="11" font-weight="700">1. DEMONSTRATE</text><line x1="40" y1="90" x2="160" y2="90" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,0.3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="100" y="110" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">Show mastery of</text><text x="100" y="125" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">existing knowledge</text><text x="100" y="145" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">in your field</text><rect x="190" y="55" width="160" height="120" rx="8" fill="#2d6a4f"/><text x="270" y="78" text-anchor="middle" fill="#c9a227" font-size="11" font-weight="700">2. ESTABLISH</text><line x1="210" y1="90" x2="330" y2="90" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,0.3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="270" y="110" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">Provide theoretical</text><text x="270" y="125" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">and empirical</text><text x="270" y="145" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">foundation</text><rect x="360" y="55" width="160" height="120" rx="8" fill="#c9a227"/><text x="440" y="78" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="11" font-weight="700">3. IDENTIFY GAP</text><line x1="380" y1="90" x2="500" y2="90" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,0.3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="440" y="110" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">Reveal the specific</text><text x="440" y="125" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">gap your study</text><text x="440" y="145" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">will address</text><rect x="60" y="210" width="420" height="50" rx="8" fill="#f0f4f8" stroke="#ddd" stroke-width="1"/><text x="270" y="235" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1a2332" font-size="10" font-weight="700">A literature review without a clear gap identification has failed its primary mission.</text><text x="270" y="250" text-anchor="middle" fill="#666" font-size="9">The gap justifies the entire research project.</text></svg><p style="font-style:italic;color:#666;font-size:.8rem;margin-top:8px;text-align:center;">Figure 1.1 The literature review serves three interconnected purposes, culminating in the identification of a research gap.</p></div>

<h2>1.2 What a Literature Review Is Not</h2>
<p>Understanding what a literature review is becomes much easier when you also understand what it is not. Several common misconceptions lead students to produce work that looks superficially like a literature review but fails to fulfil its core functions (Ridley, 2012).</p>
<p>A literature review is <strong>not an annotated bibliography</strong>. An annotated bibliography is a list of sources, each followed by a brief summary and evaluation. While annotated bibliographies can be useful preparatory tools for a literature review, they remain at the level of individual source description. A literature review goes far beyond this: it integrates sources around themes, debates, and patterns, creating a new analytical narrative that is greater than the sum of its parts (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p>A literature review is <strong>not a book report</strong>. A book report summarises what a single author said and offers a personal opinion on whether the book was good. A literature review deals with multiple sources and offers a scholarly analysis, not a personal opinion. The reviewer's role is to evaluate the methodology, validity, and contribution of each source, not to express subjective preferences (Machi and McEvoy, 2016).</p>
<p>A literature review is <strong>not a research essay</strong>. While a research essay may cite sources to support a pre-determined argument, a literature review must engage with the full range of relevant scholarship, including sources that contradict the researcher's own position. A literature review that only cites sources supporting the researcher's hypothesis is academically dishonest and will be immediately recognised as such by experienced reviewers and examiners (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>Perhaps most importantly, a literature review is <strong>not a summary</strong>. This is the single most common and damaging misconception. A summary describes what each author said. A literature review analyses, evaluates, and synthesises what the collective body of scholarship reveals about a topic. The difference is the difference between a passive recorder and an active thinker. Examiners can spot a summary masquerading as a review within the first paragraph, and it is one of the most common reasons for negative feedback on dissertations and thesis chapters (Hart, 2018).</p>

<h2>1.3 The Literature Review as an Argument</h2>
<p>If a literature review is not a summary, then what is it? The most accurate way to understand a literature review is as an argument. Like any good academic argument, a literature review has a thesis (the central claim it makes about the state of knowledge), supporting evidence (the individual studies and their findings), logical structure (the organisation of themes and sub-themes), and a conclusion (the identification of the gap that justifies new research) (Ridley, 2012).</p>
<p>The argument of a literature review typically follows a recognisable pattern. It begins by establishing the importance of the topic: why does this area of research matter? It then maps the terrain of existing knowledge: what has been studied, what has been found, what theoretical frameworks have been used? It identifies areas of agreement among researchers, but crucially, it also identifies areas of disagreement, contradiction, and debate. It evaluates the methodological strengths and weaknesses of existing studies. And it concludes by identifying what is not yet known, what has been under-researched, or what methodological limitations in previous work create opportunities for new inquiry (Machi and McEvoy, 2016).</p>
<p>This argumentative structure means that a literature review is never neutral. It always has a perspective, a position, a line of reasoning. The reviewer is not a passive conduit for other scholars' ideas but an active intellectual agent who is constructing a narrative about the state of knowledge in a field. This narrative is shaped by the reviewer's research question, which determines what literature is relevant, how it is organised, and what gaps are highlighted (Hart, 2018).</p>

<div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-header">Literature Review: Summary vs. Argument</div><div class="diagram-grid grid-2"><div class="diagram-box"><h4>Summary Approach (WRONG)</h4><p><strong>Structure:</strong> Author-by-author<br><strong>Content:</strong> "Smith (2019) found X. Jones (2020) found Y. Lee (2021) found Z."<br><strong>Voice:</strong> Passive, descriptive<br><strong>Analysis:</strong> None or minimal<br><strong>Result:</strong> A list, not a review</p></div><div class="diagram-box" style="border-top-color:var(--gold);"><h4>Argument Approach (CORRECT)</h4><p><strong>Structure:</strong> Theme-by-theme<br><strong>Content:</strong> "Research on X reveals three competing explanations (Smith, 2019; Jones, 2020), though recent evidence favours..."<br><strong>Voice:</strong> Active, analytical<br><strong>Analysis:</strong> Deep, comparative<br><strong>Result:</strong> A scholarly argument</p></div></div></div>

<h2>1.4 Functions of the Literature Review in Different Contexts</h2>
<p>The literature review plays different roles depending on the context in which it appears. In a <strong>doctoral thesis or dissertation</strong>, the literature review is typically a standalone chapter (often Chapter 2) that provides the scholarly foundation for the entire study. It is expected to be comprehensive, demonstrating that the researcher has thoroughly surveyed the relevant field. In a doctoral thesis, the literature review often runs to 15,000 to 25,000 words and represents a significant intellectual contribution in its own right (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p>In a <strong>journal article</strong>, the literature review is typically embedded in the introduction section. Due to space constraints, it is much more concise, focusing on the most directly relevant studies and using them to build a tight argument for the importance of the research question. A journal article literature review might be only 1,000 to 3,000 words, but every sentence must earn its place (Ridley, 2012).</p>
<p>In a <strong>research proposal</strong>, the literature review serves a persuasive function. It must convince reviewers that the proposed study is necessary, timely, and likely to make a significant contribution. The emphasis is on the gap and on demonstrating that the applicant understands the field well enough to conduct the proposed research (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>As a <strong>standalone publication</strong>, systematic reviews and meta-analyses are literature reviews that constitute original research in their own right. They follow rigorous protocols, use explicit and reproducible search strategies, and apply formal methods to synthesise findings across studies. We will explore these in detail in Part Six of this book (Petticrew and Roberts, 2006).</p>

<h2>1.5 The Literature Review Within the Research Process</h2>
<p>It is important to understand where the literature review fits within the broader research process. It is not a one-time activity that is completed before the study begins and then forgotten. Rather, the literature review is an ongoing process that continues throughout the research journey. Before the study begins, the literature review helps to refine the research question, identify theoretical frameworks, and justify the methodology. During data collection, new sources may emerge that require integration. During analysis, the literature review provides the context for interpreting findings. During the discussion chapter, the literature review is the benchmark against which new findings are compared (Ridley, 2012).</p>
<p>This iterative relationship between the literature review and the research process means that the literature review chapter is rarely written in one sitting. It evolves as the researcher's understanding deepens. Early drafts may be more descriptive, as the researcher is still mapping the terrain. Later drafts become more analytical and argumentative, as the researcher develops a clearer sense of their own position and contribution (Hart, 2018).</p>`;
}

function ch02() {
  return `<h1>Chapter 2</h1><h2 style='font-size:2rem;margin-bottom:4px;'>Types of Literature Reviews: Narrative, Systematic, Scoping, Meta-Analysis</h2><blockquote><strong>Learning Objectives</strong><br>By the end of this chapter, you will be able to:<br>* Identify and distinguish between the major types of literature reviews<br>* Explain the purpose, methodology, and appropriate use of narrative reviews<br>* Describe the defining features of systematic reviews and meta-analyses<br>* Understand when a scoping review is more appropriate than a systematic review<br>* Select the type of literature review most appropriate for your research context</blockquote><h2>2.1 Why Different Types of Reviews Exist</h2>
<p>Not all literature reviews are the same. Over the past several decades, the academic community has developed a range of distinct review types, each with its own methodology, purpose, and standards of rigour. The proliferation of review types reflects the growing recognition that different research questions require different approaches to synthesising existing knowledge. A clinical researcher seeking to determine the effectiveness of a drug requires a fundamentally different type of review than a social scientist exploring how the concept of "identity" has been theorised across disciplines (Grant and Booth, 2009).</p>
<p>Understanding the different types of reviews is essential for two reasons. First, if you are writing a review, you must select the type that is most appropriate for your research question and justify that choice in your methodology. Second, if you are reading reviews, you must understand what each type can and cannot tell you. A narrative review and a systematic review of the same topic will produce very different outputs, and neither is inherently superior; they simply serve different purposes (Paré et al., 2015).</p>

<div style="margin:22px 0;text-align:center;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 540 340" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto;"><text x="270" y="28" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1a2332" font-size="14" font-weight="700" font-family="Inter,sans-serif">MAJOR TYPES OF LITERATURE REVIEWS</text><rect x="30" y="55" width="480" height="55" rx="6" fill="#1a3a5c"/><text x="130" y="78" text-anchor="middle" fill="#c9a227" font-size="10" font-weight="700">NARRATIVE REVIEW</text><text x="130" y="96" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">Flexible, interpretive, broad scope</text><text x="390" y="78" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">Best for: Theoretical exploration,</text><text x="390" y="96" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">conceptual mapping, thesis chapters</text><rect x="30" y="120" width="480" height="55" rx="6" fill="#2d6a4f"/><text x="130" y="143" text-anchor="middle" fill="#c9a227" font-size="10" font-weight="700">SYSTEMATIC REVIEW</text><text x="130" y="161" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">Rigorous, reproducible, explicit protocol</text><text x="390" y="143" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">Best for: Answering specific questions,</text><text x="390" y="161" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">clinical effectiveness, policy evidence</text><rect x="30" y="185" width="480" height="55" rx="6" fill="#c9a227"/><text x="130" y="208" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="10" font-weight="700">META-ANALYSIS</text><text x="130" y="226" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">Statistical pooling of quantitative data</text><text x="390" y="208" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">Best for: Estimating overall effect size,</text><text x="390" y="226" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">resolving conflicting quantitative findings</text><rect x="30" y="250" width="480" height="55" rx="6" fill="#8b3a3a"/><text x="130" y="273" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="10" font-weight="700">SCOPING REVIEW</text><text x="130" y="291" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">Broad mapping, preliminary assessment</text><text x="390" y="273" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">Best for: Exploring extent of literature,</text><text x="390" y="291" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">identifying research priorities</text></svg><p style="font-style:italic;color:#666;font-size:.8rem;margin-top:8px;text-align:center;">Figure 2.1 The four major types of literature reviews, each serving distinct research purposes.</p></div>

<h2>2.2 The Narrative (Traditional) Literature Review</h2>
<p>The narrative review, also known as a traditional review, is the most common type of literature review in the social sciences and humanities. It provides a comprehensive, interpretive synthesis of the literature on a broad topic or research question. Unlike systematic reviews, narrative reviews do not follow a rigid, pre-specified protocol. The reviewer has considerable flexibility in selecting sources, organising themes, and interpreting findings. This flexibility is both the narrative review's greatest strength and its most significant limitation (Baumeister and Leary, 1997).</p>
<p>The strength of the narrative review lies in its ability to provide a holistic, nuanced understanding of a complex topic. The reviewer can draw on a wide range of source types, including empirical studies, theoretical papers, books, and grey literature. They can trace the historical development of ideas, compare competing theoretical frameworks, and identify subtle patterns that might be missed by more rigid approaches. Narrative reviews are particularly well-suited to topics where the literature is diverse, multidisciplinary, or theoretically complex (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>The limitation of the narrative review is its susceptibility to bias. Because the selection of sources is not governed by explicit, reproducible criteria, two reviewers could produce very different narrative reviews of the same topic, each emphasising different sources and reaching different conclusions. This subjectivity means that narrative reviews are considered weaker evidence than systematic reviews in fields where evidence hierarchies are used, such as medicine and health sciences (Grant and Booth, 2009).</p>
<p>Despite these limitations, the narrative review remains the standard approach for thesis and dissertation literature review chapters. When your goal is to demonstrate comprehensive knowledge of a field, provide theoretical context for your study, and identify a research gap, the narrative review is almost always the appropriate choice (Hart, 2018).</p>

<h2>2.3 The Systematic Review</h2>
<p>A systematic review is a highly structured, protocol-driven approach to reviewing the literature. It uses explicit, pre-defined criteria for searching, selecting, evaluating, and synthesising research evidence. The defining feature of a systematic review is its emphasis on transparency and reproducibility: every step of the process is documented so that another researcher could replicate the review and arrive at the same body of included studies (Petticrew and Roberts, 2006).</p>
<p>Systematic reviews typically begin with a clearly formulated research question, often structured using the PICO framework (Population, Intervention, Comparison, Outcome) or similar models. The reviewer then develops a search strategy specifying which databases will be searched, what search terms and Boolean operators will be used, and what date range will be covered. Explicit inclusion and exclusion criteria are defined before the search begins, specifying the types of studies, populations, interventions, and outcomes that are eligible for inclusion (Higgins and Thomas, 2019).</p>
<p>After the search is conducted, the results are screened in two stages. First, titles and abstracts are reviewed to exclude clearly irrelevant studies. Second, the full texts of potentially relevant studies are retrieved and assessed against the inclusion criteria. This process is typically documented using a PRISMA flow diagram, which visually tracks the number of studies identified, screened, excluded, and included (Moher et al., 2009).</p>
<p>Systematic reviews are considered the gold standard of evidence synthesis in many fields, particularly medicine, public health, and education. They are most appropriate when the research question is specific and focused, when the relevant literature consists primarily of empirical studies with comparable methodologies, and when the goal is to provide a definitive answer to a question about effectiveness or association (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>

<h2>2.4 Meta-Analysis</h2>
<p>A meta-analysis is a statistical technique that can be applied within the framework of a systematic review. While a systematic review synthesises findings qualitatively (through narrative description), a meta-analysis synthesises them quantitatively by pooling the statistical results from multiple studies to calculate an overall effect size. The result is a single, statistically powerful estimate of the effect that is based on a much larger combined sample than any individual study could achieve (Borenstein et al., 2021).</p>
<p>Meta-analysis is only appropriate when the included studies are sufficiently similar in their design, populations, interventions, and outcome measures to justify statistical pooling. If the studies are too heterogeneous, pooling their results produces a meaningless average. Assessing and reporting heterogeneity (typically using the I-squared statistic) is a critical component of any meta-analysis (Higgins and Thomas, 2019).</p>
<p>The primary outputs of a meta-analysis are the overall effect size (typically expressed as a standardised mean difference, odds ratio, or risk ratio), a confidence interval, and a forest plot that visually displays the effect size and confidence interval for each included study alongside the pooled estimate. These outputs provide a clear, quantitative answer to the research question that is far more robust than the findings of any single study (Borenstein et al., 2021).</p>

<h2>2.5 The Scoping Review</h2>
<p>A scoping review occupies a middle ground between the flexibility of a narrative review and the rigour of a systematic review. Scoping reviews are used to map the existing literature on a broad topic, identify key concepts, types of evidence, and gaps in the research, and determine whether a full systematic review is warranted. They are particularly useful for emerging fields where the literature is diverse and has not yet been comprehensively reviewed (Arksey and O'Malley, 2005).</p>
<p>Unlike systematic reviews, scoping reviews do not typically assess the quality of included studies, and they do not aim to produce a definitive answer to a specific question. Instead, they aim to provide an overview of the volume, nature, and characteristics of the available literature. Scoping reviews follow a structured methodology (typically the Arksey and O'Malley framework, refined by Levac, Colquhoun, and O'Brien, 2010), but they are more iterative and flexible than systematic reviews in their approach to searching and selecting sources.</p>
<p>Scoping reviews are increasingly popular in the social sciences and health sciences, where researchers often need to understand the breadth of a literature before formulating a specific, answerable research question. They are also useful for identifying conceptual boundaries, clarifying working definitions, and highlighting methodological approaches used in a field (Peters et al., 2020).</p>

<h2>2.6 Choosing the Right Type of Review</h2>
<p>Selecting the appropriate type of review is a design decision that must be justified in your methodology. The choice depends on your research question, your discipline's norms, and the nature of the available literature. If your goal is to provide a comprehensive, interpretive synthesis for a thesis chapter, a narrative review is almost always appropriate. If your goal is to answer a specific question about effectiveness or association, a systematic review (with or without meta-analysis) is the gold standard. If your goal is to map a broad or emerging field, a scoping review is the best fit (Grant and Booth, 2009).</p>
<p>Regardless of the type you choose, the principles of thorough searching, critical evaluation, and analytical synthesis apply to all forms of literature review. The type determines the methodology, but the underlying intellectual skills are the same (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>`;
}

function ch03() {
  return `<h1>Chapter 3</h1><h2 style='font-size:2rem;margin-bottom:4px;'>The Purpose of the Literature Review in Different Research Contexts</h2><blockquote><strong>Learning Objectives</strong><br>By the end of this chapter, you will be able to:<br>* Explain how the literature review functions differently in quantitative, qualitative, and mixed methods research<br>* Describe the role of the literature review in establishing a theoretical framework<br>* Understand how the literature review informs the conceptual framework of a study<br>* Articulate the relationship between the literature review and hypothesis development<br>* Adapt your literature review approach to your specific research paradigm</blockquote><h2>3.1 The Literature Review in Quantitative Research</h2>
<p>In quantitative research, the literature review plays a specific and clearly defined role. It provides the empirical and theoretical basis for the study, identifies the variables to be measured, justifies the hypotheses to be tested, and establishes the significance of the research problem. The quantitative literature review is typically deductive: it moves from the general body of existing knowledge to the specific hypotheses that the study will test (Creswell and Creswell, 2018).</p>
<p>The literature review in a quantitative study must accomplish several tasks. First, it must identify and define the key variables in the study. Every variable in a quantitative study must be grounded in the literature: the researcher must demonstrate that the variable has been studied before, explain how it has been conceptualised and measured, and justify the operational definitions used in the current study. Second, the literature review must provide the logical basis for each hypothesis. A hypothesis is not a guess; it is a prediction based on existing evidence. The literature review should make each hypothesis seem inevitable, the logical conclusion of the evidence presented (Creswell and Creswell, 2018).</p>
<p>Third, the literature review in quantitative research must identify the theoretical framework that underpins the study. A theoretical framework is an established theory or model that provides the lens through which the researcher views the phenomena under investigation. For example, a study of employee motivation might use Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory or Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs as its theoretical framework. The literature review explains the theory, justifies its selection, and shows how it relates to the variables being studied (Grant and Osanloo, 2014).</p>

<div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-header">The Literature Review's Role Across Research Paradigms</div><div class="diagram-grid grid-3"><div class="diagram-box"><h4>Quantitative</h4><p><strong>Timing:</strong> Before data collection<br><strong>Purpose:</strong> Justify hypotheses, define variables<br><strong>Framework:</strong> Theoretical framework<br><strong>Approach:</strong> Deductive<br><strong>Gap:</strong> Specific, measurable</p></div><div class="diagram-box" style="border-top-color:var(--gold);"><h4>Qualitative</h4><p><strong>Timing:</strong> Before and during study<br><strong>Purpose:</strong> Contextualise, sensitise<br><strong>Framework:</strong> Conceptual framework<br><strong>Approach:</strong> Inductive or iterative<br><strong>Gap:</strong> Experiential, interpretive</p></div><div class="diagram-box" style="border-top-color:var(--teal);"><h4>Mixed Methods</h4><p><strong>Timing:</strong> Throughout<br><strong>Purpose:</strong> Integrate both functions<br><strong>Framework:</strong> Both types combined<br><strong>Approach:</strong> Pragmatic<br><strong>Gap:</strong> Multi-dimensional</p></div></div></div>

<h2>3.2 The Literature Review in Qualitative Research</h2>
<p>The role of the literature review in qualitative research is more complex and more debated than in quantitative research. Some qualitative traditions, particularly grounded theory, argue that an extensive literature review before data collection can bias the researcher by imposing pre-existing categories and expectations on the data. Glaser (1992) famously warned researchers to avoid extensive literature reviews before entering the field, arguing that the review should emerge alongside the data analysis, not precede it.</p>
<p>However, this position has been significantly modified in contemporary practice. Most qualitative researchers now agree that some engagement with the literature before data collection is not only acceptable but necessary. The literature review helps the researcher to understand the broader context of the phenomenon under study, to refine the research question, and to demonstrate to supervisors and ethics committees that the study is grounded in existing scholarship. The key difference from quantitative research is that the qualitative literature review is more exploratory and less prescriptive. It sensitises the researcher to important concepts and themes without dictating what the researcher should find (Charmaz, 2014).</p>
<p>In qualitative research, the literature review often helps to establish a <strong>conceptual framework</strong> rather than a theoretical framework. A conceptual framework is a visual or narrative representation of the key concepts the researcher expects to encounter and the anticipated relationships between them. Unlike a theoretical framework, which applies an established theory, a conceptual framework is constructed by the researcher from the literature and may evolve as the study progresses (Ravitch and Riggan, 2017).</p>
<p>In phenomenological research, the literature review provides the context for understanding the lived experience being explored, but the researcher must bracket (set aside) pre-existing knowledge during data collection to allow the participants' experiences to speak for themselves. In case study research, the literature review provides the theoretical propositions that guide data collection and analysis. In each case, the literature review serves the broader study, but the specific relationship varies by tradition (Creswell and Poth, 2018).</p>

<h2>3.3 The Literature Review in Mixed Methods Research</h2>
<p>In mixed methods research, the literature review must serve the needs of both the quantitative and qualitative components of the study. This typically means reviewing two bodies of literature: the empirical literature relevant to the quantitative variables and hypotheses, and the theoretical and conceptual literature relevant to the qualitative exploration. The literature review must also justify the use of a mixed methods approach by demonstrating that neither quantitative nor qualitative methods alone would be sufficient to address the research question (Creswell and Plano Clark, 2018).</p>
<p>The placement and structure of the literature review in a mixed methods study depends on the specific design. In a convergent design, where quantitative and qualitative data are collected simultaneously, the literature review typically appears as a single, integrated chapter. In a sequential design, where one phase informs the next, the literature review may be distributed across the study, with the initial review informing the first phase and additional literature being reviewed between phases (Tashakkori and Teddlie, 2010).</p>

<h2>3.4 Theoretical Frameworks and Conceptual Frameworks</h2>
<p>Two terms that cause considerable confusion are "theoretical framework" and "conceptual framework." While they are related, they are not interchangeable. A <strong>theoretical framework</strong> is an existing, established theory that the researcher adopts as the lens for their study. It is borrowed from the literature and applied to the specific research context. Examples include Social Cognitive Theory, the Technology Acceptance Model, or Bronfenbrenner's Ecological Systems Theory (Grant and Osanloo, 2014).</p>
<p>A <strong>conceptual framework</strong> is constructed by the researcher from the literature. It represents the researcher's own understanding of the key concepts and their relationships, synthesised from multiple sources. A conceptual framework is not a theory in itself; it is a map of the conceptual territory that the study will explore. It may incorporate elements from several theories and integrate them with empirical findings to create a bespoke framework for the specific study (Ravitch and Riggan, 2017).</p>
<p>The literature review is the vehicle through which both types of framework are developed and presented. Without a thorough engagement with the literature, neither a theoretical framework nor a conceptual framework can be adequately constructed or justified (Creswell and Creswell, 2018).</p>

<h2>3.5 From Literature to Hypotheses and Research Questions</h2>
<p>In quantitative research, the literature review should lead directly and logically to the study's hypotheses. Each hypothesis should be a specific, testable prediction that emerges from the patterns, gaps, or contradictions identified in the literature. The reader should be able to trace the logical thread from the reviewed literature to each hypothesis without encountering any logical leaps (Creswell and Creswell, 2018).</p>
<p>In qualitative research, the literature review leads to the refinement of the research question rather than the formulation of hypotheses. The literature helps the researcher to narrow their focus, identify the aspects of the phenomenon that are least understood, and frame a question that is genuinely exploratory rather than confirmatory. The qualitative research question should emerge from the literature as the most important question that the existing body of knowledge has not yet answered (Ravitch and Riggan, 2017).</p>`;
}

function ch04() {
  return `<h1>Chapter 4</h1><h2 style='font-size:2rem;margin-bottom:4px;'>Common Literature Review Mistakes That Examiners Hate</h2><blockquote><strong>Learning Objectives</strong><br>By the end of this chapter, you will be able to:<br>* Identify the most common structural and analytical mistakes in literature reviews<br>* Understand why descriptive summaries fail to satisfy examiner expectations<br>* Recognise and avoid the "laundry list" and "hero worship" approaches<br>* Ensure your literature review demonstrates critical thinking and synthesis<br>* Apply a self-assessment checklist to evaluate your own review before submission</blockquote><h2>4.1 The Summary Trap</h2>
<p>The single most common mistake in literature reviews, the one that examiners encounter more than any other, is the failure to move beyond summary to synthesis and critical analysis. This mistake transforms what should be a scholarly argument into what reads like a series of book reports. The resulting text follows a predictable and deeply unsatisfying pattern: "Smith (2018) studied X and found Y. Jones (2019) investigated A and found B. Williams (2020) examined C and concluded D." Each source is described in isolation, with no attempt to compare, contrast, or integrate findings across sources (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p>This approach fails because it demonstrates reading but not thinking. The examiner already knows that the student can read; what they want to see is that the student can analyse, evaluate, and synthesise. A literature review that merely summarises individual sources tells the examiner nothing about the student's intellectual capabilities and provides no evidence that they understand the broader patterns, debates, and gaps in the field (Ridley, 2012).</p>
<p>The fix is to organise the review thematically rather than source-by-source. Instead of devoting a paragraph to each source, the reviewer should identify the key themes, debates, or findings in the literature and discuss multiple sources within each theme. The focus shifts from "what did Author X say?" to "what does the literature collectively tell us about Theme Y?" This thematic approach forces synthesis and makes it impossible to simply list sources without integrating them (Machi and McEvoy, 2016).</p>

<div style="margin:22px 0;text-align:center;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 540 320" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto;"><text x="270" y="28" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1a2332" font-size="14" font-weight="700" font-family="Inter,sans-serif">THE SEVEN DEADLY SINS OF LITERATURE REVIEWS</text><rect x="30" y="55" width="230" height="35" rx="6" fill="#8b3a3a"/><text x="145" y="77" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="9.5" font-weight="600">1. Summary without synthesis</text><rect x="280" y="55" width="230" height="35" rx="6" fill="#8b3a3a"/><text x="395" y="77" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="9.5" font-weight="600">2. Source-by-source structure</text><rect x="30" y="100" width="230" height="35" rx="6" fill="#8b3a3a"/><text x="145" y="122" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="9.5" font-weight="600">3. No critical evaluation</text><rect x="280" y="100" width="230" height="35" rx="6" fill="#8b3a3a"/><text x="395" y="122" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="9.5" font-weight="600">4. Missing gap identification</text><rect x="30" y="145" width="230" height="35" rx="6" fill="#8b3a3a"/><text x="145" y="167" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="9.5" font-weight="600">5. Over-reliance on few sources</text><rect x="280" y="145" width="230" height="35" rx="6" fill="#8b3a3a"/><text x="395" y="167" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="9.5" font-weight="600">6. Ignoring contradictory evidence</text><rect x="155" y="190" width="230" height="35" rx="6" fill="#8b3a3a"/><text x="270" y="212" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="9.5" font-weight="600">7. No connection to your study</text><rect x="60" y="250" width="420" height="50" rx="8" fill="#f0f4f8" stroke="#ddd" stroke-width="1"/><text x="270" y="272" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1a2332" font-size="10" font-weight="700">Every one of these mistakes signals a failure of critical thinking,</text><text x="270" y="288" text-anchor="middle" fill="#666" font-size="9">not a failure of effort. Hard work does not compensate for poor analysis.</text></svg><p style="font-style:italic;color:#666;font-size:.8rem;margin-top:8px;text-align:center;">Figure 4.1 The seven most common literature review mistakes, each capable of undermining an otherwise competent study.</p></div>

<h2>4.2 The Laundry List</h2>
<p>A close relative of the summary trap is the "laundry list" approach. This occurs when the reviewer organises the literature chronologically or alphabetically, producing a text that reads like a catalogue rather than an analysis. "In 2015, Author A published a study on X. In 2016, Author B published a study on Y. In 2017, Author C published a study on Z." This approach provides no analytical framework and no interpretive lens. It tells the reader what was published, but not what the collective body of research means (Ridley, 2012).</p>
<p>The chronological approach can be legitimate in limited circumstances, such as when tracing the historical development of a specific concept or debate. However, even when chronology is used as an organising principle, it must be combined with thematic analysis. The reviewer must explain why the literature developed in the way it did, what each era or phase contributed to current understanding, and how the trajectory of research has created the current state of knowledge (Hart, 2018).</p>

<h2>4.3 Hero Worship and Over-Reliance</h2>
<p>Another common mistake is what might be called "hero worship," where the reviewer treats a particular author or study as the definitive authority on the topic and structures the entire review around their work. While seminal works deserve prominent attention, a literature review that relies too heavily on a single author or study is inherently limited. It suggests that the reviewer has not read widely enough and that their understanding of the field is filtered through a single perspective (Machi and McEvoy, 2016).</p>
<p>A related problem is over-reliance on a small number of sources. If a 10,000-word literature review cites only 15 sources, the examiner will immediately question the thoroughness of the search. While there is no magic number of sources that is "enough" (it depends on the topic and the field), a doctoral literature review typically engages with 100 to 300 sources, and a master's level review with 40 to 100. These numbers are guidelines, not rules, but a review that falls significantly below them will raise concerns (Hart, 2018).</p>

<h2>4.4 Absence of Critical Evaluation</h2>
<p>A literature review that describes what previous studies found without evaluating how they found it is incomplete. Critical evaluation means assessing the methodological quality of each study: Was the sample appropriate? Were the measures valid and reliable? Were the statistical analyses correctly applied? Were the conclusions justified by the evidence? Were there ethical concerns? This evaluation is not about being negative; it is about being scholarly (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>Critical evaluation serves two purposes. First, it helps the reviewer to determine how much weight to give each study's findings. A finding from a well-designed, large-scale randomised controlled trial should carry more weight than a finding from a small, convenience-sampled survey with no control group. Second, methodological weaknesses in existing studies often point to the gap that the reviewer's study will address. If all existing studies of a phenomenon use quantitative surveys, a qualitative study might provide the deeper understanding that surveys cannot capture (Ridley, 2012).</p>

<h2>4.5 No Connection to Your Own Study</h2>
<p>The literature review must always serve the broader purpose of your research project. Every source included, every theme discussed, every finding presented must connect to the research question you are investigating. A common mistake is to include interesting but irrelevant material simply because the reviewer read it and found it fascinating. If a source does not directly contribute to the argument leading to your research gap, it does not belong in the review, no matter how intellectually stimulating it may be (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p>The literature review should end with a clear, explicit statement of the research gap. This statement should flow naturally from the preceding analysis and should make the reader feel that the proposed study is the inevitable next step. If the reader reaches the end of the literature review and does not understand exactly why the proposed study is necessary, the review has failed its most important function (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>

<h2>4.6 Self-Assessment Checklist</h2>
<p>Before submitting your literature review, assess it against the following criteria. Is the review organised thematically rather than source-by-source? Does it synthesise rather than merely summarise? Does it critically evaluate the methodology of key studies? Does it identify agreements, contradictions, and gaps? Does every paragraph serve the overarching argument? Does it conclude with a clear statement of the research gap? Is every source cited relevant to your research question? If you can answer yes to all of these questions, your literature review is on the right track. If not, you know exactly where to focus your revisions (Ridley, 2012).</p>`;
}

function ch05() {
  return `<h1>Chapter 5</h1><h2 style='font-size:2rem;margin-bottom:4px;'>Defining the Scope and Boundaries of Your Review</h2><blockquote><strong>Learning Objectives</strong><br>By the end of this chapter, you will be able to:<br>* Explain why setting clear boundaries is essential for a manageable literature review<br>* Define the scope of a literature review using conceptual, temporal, and geographical boundaries<br>* Use your research question as the primary tool for determining relevance<br>* Develop explicit inclusion and exclusion criteria for your search<br>* Avoid the twin dangers of too broad and too narrow scope</blockquote><h2>5.1 The Problem of Scope</h2>
<p>One of the first challenges any researcher faces when beginning a literature review is determining its scope. The body of academic literature is vast and growing exponentially. Even a relatively narrow topic can yield thousands of potentially relevant sources across multiple databases. Without clear boundaries, the researcher risks drowning in an ocean of papers, becoming paralysed by the sheer volume of material, and never progressing beyond the reading phase to the writing phase. The anxiety of comprehensiveness, the fear that you might miss something important, is one of the most common causes of literature review paralysis (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p>Setting boundaries is not an admission of defeat; it is a methodological necessity. Every literature review, from a brief journal article introduction to a comprehensive doctoral thesis chapter, must have limits. The key is to set those limits deliberately, transparently, and in a way that can be justified to examiners and reviewers. The scope of your review should be wide enough to capture the essential body of relevant scholarship, but narrow enough to be manageable and focused (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>

<h2>5.2 Using Your Research Question to Define Scope</h2>
<p>Your research question is the single most important tool for defining the scope of your literature review. If a source does not contribute to answering your research question, it is outside the scope of your review. This principle sounds simple, but applying it consistently requires discipline. It is tempting to include sources that are interesting, well-written, or authored by famous scholars, even if they are only tangentially related to your question. Resist this temptation. A focused review is always stronger than a broad one (Ridley, 2012).</p>
<p>To use your research question effectively as a scoping tool, break it down into its component concepts. For example, if your research question is "How does remote working affect employee wellbeing in the technology sector?", the component concepts are: remote working, employee wellbeing, and the technology sector. Your review must cover the literature on each of these concepts and their intersections. Literature on remote working in healthcare, while interesting, is outside your scope. Literature on employee wellbeing in face-to-face settings is relevant only insofar as it provides a comparison or theoretical foundation (Hart, 2018).</p>

<div style="margin:22px 0;text-align:center;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 540 300" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto;"><text x="270" y="28" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1a2332" font-size="14" font-weight="700" font-family="Inter,sans-serif">DEFINING SCOPE: THREE BOUNDARY TYPES</text><rect x="20" y="55" width="160" height="150" rx="8" fill="#1a3a5c"/><text x="100" y="78" text-anchor="middle" fill="#c9a227" font-size="11" font-weight="700">CONCEPTUAL</text><line x1="40" y1="90" x2="160" y2="90" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,0.3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="100" y="112" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">Which topics, theories,</text><text x="100" y="127" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">and variables are</text><text x="100" y="142" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">included or excluded?</text><text x="100" y="170" text-anchor="middle" fill="#c9a227" font-size="9" font-weight="600">Driven by your</text><text x="100" y="185" text-anchor="middle" fill="#c9a227" font-size="9" font-weight="600">research question</text><rect x="190" y="55" width="160" height="150" rx="8" fill="#2d6a4f"/><text x="270" y="78" text-anchor="middle" fill="#c9a227" font-size="11" font-weight="700">TEMPORAL</text><line x1="210" y1="90" x2="330" y2="90" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,0.3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="270" y="112" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">What time period</text><text x="270" y="127" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">does the review</text><text x="270" y="142" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">cover?</text><text x="270" y="170" text-anchor="middle" fill="#c9a227" font-size="9" font-weight="600">Usually 10-20 years</text><text x="270" y="185" text-anchor="middle" fill="#c9a227" font-size="9" font-weight="600">+ seminal works</text><rect x="360" y="55" width="160" height="150" rx="8" fill="#c9a227"/><text x="440" y="78" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="11" font-weight="700">GEOGRAPHICAL</text><line x1="380" y1="90" x2="500" y2="90" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,0.3)" stroke-width="1"/><text x="440" y="112" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">Which countries or</text><text x="440" y="127" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">regions are included</text><text x="440" y="142" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">or excluded?</text><text x="440" y="170" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9" font-weight="600">Context-dependent:</text><text x="440" y="185" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9" font-weight="600">justify your choice</text><rect x="60" y="230" width="420" height="40" rx="8" fill="#f0f4f8" stroke="#ddd" stroke-width="1"/><text x="270" y="255" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1a2332" font-size="10" font-weight="700">All boundaries must be explicitly stated and justified in your methodology.</text></svg><p style="font-style:italic;color:#666;font-size:.8rem;margin-top:8px;text-align:center;">Figure 5.1 Three types of boundaries that define the scope of a literature review.</p></div>

<h2>5.3 Conceptual Boundaries</h2>
<p>Conceptual boundaries define which topics, theories, and variables are within the scope of your review and which are excluded. These boundaries should be derived directly from your research question and its component concepts. For each concept in your research question, you should decide how broadly or narrowly it will be defined. For example, does "employee wellbeing" include physical health, mental health, job satisfaction, work-life balance, or all of these? The answer depends on how the concept is operationalised in your study and what the existing literature reveals about its dimensions (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>Conceptual boundaries also extend to the types of sources you will include. Will you include only empirical studies, or also theoretical and conceptual papers? Will you include grey literature (reports, policy documents, working papers), or only peer-reviewed journal articles? Will you include studies from all disciplines, or only from your own? Each of these decisions narrows the scope of your review and must be justified (Hart, 2018).</p>

<h2>5.4 Temporal and Geographical Boundaries</h2>
<p>Temporal boundaries define the time period your review covers. In most fields, a literature review focuses on the most recent 10 to 20 years of scholarship, with exceptions for seminal or foundational works that are older. The justification for temporal boundaries is that science evolves, and older studies may use outdated methods, definitions, or theoretical frameworks. However, some topics require a longer historical perspective, particularly when tracing the development of a concept or theory over time (Ridley, 2012).</p>
<p>Geographical boundaries define the countries or regions from which studies are included. In some fields, research findings are considered universal, and geographical boundaries are less important. In others, particularly in the social sciences, context matters enormously, and a study conducted in Scandinavia may have limited applicability to sub-Saharan Africa. If your own study is conducted in a specific country, you should prioritise literature from that context while also drawing on international research to provide broader perspective (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>

<h2>5.5 Avoiding Common Scoping Errors</h2>
<p>Two common errors sit at opposite ends of the scoping spectrum. A scope that is <strong>too broad</strong> results in a literature review that is superficial, unfocused, and unmanageable. The reviewer tries to cover everything and ends up covering nothing in depth. The result is a descriptive survey that skims the surface of dozens of sub-topics without providing a deep understanding of any of them (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p>A scope that is <strong>too narrow</strong> results in a literature review that is thin and insufficiently contextualised. The reviewer focuses so tightly on their specific research question that they fail to engage with the broader theoretical and empirical context that gives their study meaning. The result is a review that demonstrates technical competence but lacks scholarly depth (Ridley, 2012).</p>
<p>The ideal scope is one that is broad enough to demonstrate comprehensive knowledge of the field and narrow enough to maintain analytical depth and focus. Finding this balance requires iterative refinement. Start with a broad initial search to understand the landscape, then progressively narrow your focus as your understanding deepens and your research question crystallises (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>`;
}

function ch06() {
  return `<h1>Chapter 6</h1><h2 style='font-size:2rem;margin-bottom:4px;'>Developing Your Search Strategy and Keywords</h2><blockquote><strong>Learning Objectives</strong><br>By the end of this chapter, you will be able to:<br>* Develop a systematic, documented search strategy for your literature review<br>* Identify and refine keywords and search terms from your research question<br>* Use Boolean operators (AND, OR, NOT) to construct effective database searches<br>* Apply truncation, wildcards, and phrase searching to improve search precision<br>* Document your search strategy for transparency and reproducibility</blockquote><h2>6.1 Why a Search Strategy Matters</h2>
<p>The quality of a literature review depends entirely on the quality of the literature search that underpins it. If your search is unsystematic, haphazard, or biased, your review will reflect those flaws. You will miss important studies, over-represent certain perspectives, and reach conclusions that are not supported by the full body of available evidence. A systematic search strategy ensures that your review is comprehensive, balanced, and defensible (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>A search strategy is a documented plan that specifies what you will search for, where you will search, and how you will search. It includes the databases you will use, the keywords and search terms you will enter, the Boolean operators and other search techniques you will apply, and the inclusion and exclusion criteria you will use to select relevant studies. Documenting this strategy allows others to replicate your search and allows your examiners to evaluate the thoroughness of your literature identification process (Petticrew and Roberts, 2006).</p>

<h2>6.2 From Research Question to Search Terms</h2>
<p>The first step in developing a search strategy is to extract the key concepts from your research question and translate them into search terms. This process is not as straightforward as it seems, because the language of research questions does not always match the language used in database indexing or by other researchers. The same concept may be described using different terms in different disciplines, time periods, or geographical contexts (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p>For each key concept, develop a list of synonyms, related terms, and alternative spellings. For example, if your concept is "employee wellbeing," related terms might include "worker wellness," "occupational health," "job satisfaction," "workplace mental health," and "employee welfare." If your concept is "remote working," alternatives might include "telecommuting," "teleworking," "work from home," "distributed work," and "virtual work." The more comprehensive your list of alternative terms, the more comprehensive your search will be (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>

<div style="margin:22px 0;text-align:center;"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 540 320" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto;"><text x="270" y="28" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1a2332" font-size="14" font-weight="700" font-family="Inter,sans-serif">BOOLEAN OPERATORS: THE BUILDING BLOCKS OF SEARCH</text><rect x="30" y="55" width="480" height="70" rx="6" fill="#2d6a4f"/><text x="100" y="78" text-anchor="middle" fill="#c9a227" font-size="12" font-weight="700">AND</text><text x="100" y="98" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">Narrows search</text><text x="350" y="78" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="10">"remote work" AND "employee wellbeing"</text><text x="350" y="98" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.7)" font-size="9">Returns only results containing BOTH terms</text><rect x="30" y="135" width="480" height="70" rx="6" fill="#1a3a5c"/><text x="100" y="158" text-anchor="middle" fill="#c9a227" font-size="12" font-weight="700">OR</text><text x="100" y="178" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">Broadens search</text><text x="350" y="158" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="10">"remote work" OR "telecommuting" OR "telework"</text><text x="350" y="178" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.7)" font-size="9">Returns results containing ANY of the terms</text><rect x="30" y="215" width="480" height="70" rx="6" fill="#8b3a3a"/><text x="100" y="238" text-anchor="middle" fill="#c9a227" font-size="12" font-weight="700">NOT</text><text x="100" y="258" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.85)" font-size="9">Excludes terms</text><text x="350" y="238" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-size="10">"remote work" NOT "freelancing"</text><text x="350" y="258" text-anchor="middle" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.7)" font-size="9">Excludes results containing the excluded term</text></svg><p style="font-style:italic;color:#666;font-size:.8rem;margin-top:8px;text-align:center;">Figure 6.1 Boolean operators combine search terms to control the breadth and precision of database searches.</p></div>

<h2>6.3 Boolean Operators</h2>
<p><strong>Boolean operators</strong> are logical connectors that combine search terms to control the breadth and focus of a database search. The three primary Boolean operators are AND, OR, and NOT. Understanding how they work is essential for conducting effective searches (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p><strong>AND</strong> narrows a search by requiring that all connected terms appear in the results. Searching for "remote work AND employee wellbeing" will return only articles that contain both concepts. The more terms you connect with AND, the narrower and more focused your results will be. <strong>OR</strong> broadens a search by accepting any of the connected terms. Searching for "telecommuting OR telework OR remote work" will return articles that use any of these terms, capturing studies that might use different vocabulary for the same concept. <strong>NOT</strong> excludes specific terms from the results. Searching for "remote work NOT freelancing" will exclude articles about freelancing, which may use the term "remote work" in a different context (Petticrew and Roberts, 2006).</p>
<p>An effective search strategy typically combines these operators in a structured search string. The general principle is to use OR within each concept (to capture synonyms) and AND between concepts (to ensure relevance). For example: ("remote work" OR "telecommuting" OR "telework") AND ("employee wellbeing" OR "worker wellness" OR "job satisfaction") AND ("technology sector" OR "IT industry" OR "tech companies") (Hart, 2018).</p>

<h2>6.4 Truncation, Wildcards, and Phrase Searching</h2>
<p><strong>Truncation</strong> allows you to search for all words that share a common root. In most databases, the truncation symbol is an asterisk (*). For example, searching for "organis*" will retrieve articles containing "organisation," "organisational," "organise," "organising," and so on. This is particularly useful for capturing both British and American spellings and for finding all forms of a word without listing each one individually (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p><strong>Wildcards</strong> replace a single character within a word. In most databases, the wildcard symbol is a question mark (?). For example, searching for "wom?n" will retrieve results for both "woman" and "women." <strong>Phrase searching</strong> uses quotation marks to search for an exact sequence of words. Searching for "work-life balance" as a phrase ensures that the database returns only results where these three words appear together in this exact order, rather than returning any article that contains the words "work," "life," and "balance" somewhere in the text (Hart, 2018).</p>

<h2>6.5 Documenting Your Search</h2>
<p>A well-documented search strategy serves several purposes. It demonstrates to examiners that your search was systematic and thorough. It allows other researchers to replicate your search and verify your findings. And it provides a record that you can refer back to if you need to update or extend your search later in the research process (Petticrew and Roberts, 2006).</p>
<p>For each database searched, document the following: the name of the database, the date the search was conducted, the exact search string used (including all Boolean operators, truncation, and phrase searching), any filters applied (such as date range, language, or publication type), and the number of results returned. This documentation is typically presented as a table in the methodology section of your review or as an appendix (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>`;
}

function ch07() {
  return `<h1>Chapter 7</h1><h2 style='font-size:2rem;margin-bottom:4px;'>Conducting a Systematic and Reproducible Database Search</h2><blockquote><strong>Learning Objectives</strong><br>By the end of this chapter, you will be able to:<br>* Identify and access the major academic databases relevant to your discipline<br>* Conduct structured searches across multiple databases using a consistent strategy<br>* Use database-specific features such as subject headings, filters, and advanced search interfaces<br>* Supplement database searches with citation tracking and hand-searching<br>* Manage search results using reference management software</blockquote><h2>7.1 Choosing the Right Databases</h2>
<p>The first practical decision in conducting a literature search is selecting the databases to search. No single database indexes all academic publications, so a comprehensive search requires searching multiple databases. The choice of databases depends on your discipline and the nature of your research question. For social sciences research, Scopus, Web of Science, and PsycINFO are typically essential. For health research, PubMed and CINAHL are critical. For education, ERIC is indispensable. For business and management, Business Source Complete and ABI/INFORM are the key resources (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>In addition to discipline-specific databases, Google Scholar serves as a useful supplementary tool. Its primary advantage is its extremely broad coverage: it indexes journal articles, conference papers, theses, books, preprints, and many other source types across all disciplines. However, its search algorithm is opaque, its quality control is minimal, and its results are not easily reproducible. Google Scholar should be used to supplement a structured database search, not to replace it (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p>For a doctoral thesis, it is standard practice to search at least three to five databases, including at least one multidisciplinary database (such as Scopus or Web of Science) and at least one or two discipline-specific databases. The choice must be justified in your methodology, with a clear explanation of why each database was selected and what type of literature it captures (Petticrew and Roberts, 2006).</p>

<h2>7.2 Navigating Database Interfaces</h2>
<p>Each database has its own interface, its own search features, and its own indexing system. Learning to navigate these differences is a practical skill that saves enormous time and produces significantly better results. Most databases offer both a basic search (a single search box) and an advanced search (multiple fields with Boolean connectors). For academic research, the advanced search should always be used, as it provides much greater control over the search strategy (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>Many databases use <strong>subject headings</strong> (also called thesaurus terms, MeSH terms in PubMed, or descriptors in ERIC) to categorise articles by topic. These are standardised terms applied by human indexers that describe the subject matter of each article. Searching by subject heading is more precise than searching by keyword, because it overcomes the problem of different authors using different words for the same concept. For example, a subject heading search for "Telecommuting" in a database will retrieve all articles indexed under that heading, regardless of whether the article text uses the word "telecommuting," "remote work," or "working from home" (Hart, 2018).</p>

<div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-header">Database Search: Basic vs. Advanced</div><div class="diagram-grid grid-2"><div class="diagram-box"><h4>Basic Search</h4><p><strong>Interface:</strong> Single search box<br><strong>Control:</strong> Minimal, relies on algorithm<br><strong>Precision:</strong> Low, many irrelevant results<br><strong>Reproducibility:</strong> Poor<br><strong>Use for:</strong> Initial exploration only</p></div><div class="diagram-box" style="border-top-color:var(--gold);"><h4>Advanced Search</h4><p><strong>Interface:</strong> Multiple fields + Boolean<br><strong>Control:</strong> Full control over terms and fields<br><strong>Precision:</strong> High, targeted results<br><strong>Reproducibility:</strong> Excellent<br><strong>Use for:</strong> All formal literature reviews</p></div></div></div>

<h2>7.3 Supplementary Search Techniques</h2>
<p>Database searches, no matter how comprehensive, will not identify all relevant literature. Several supplementary techniques are essential for ensuring thorough coverage. <strong>Citation tracking</strong> (also called snowballing) involves examining the reference lists of key articles to identify earlier studies that informed them (backward citation tracking) and using citation databases to identify later studies that cited them (forward citation tracking). This technique is particularly effective for identifying seminal works and for finding studies that may not be well-indexed in databases (Greenhalgh and Peacock, 2005).</p>
<p><strong>Hand-searching</strong> involves manually browsing the tables of contents of the most relevant journals in your field for the most recent issues. This catches articles that have been published too recently to be fully indexed in databases. <strong>Grey literature searching</strong> involves searching for reports, working papers, conference proceedings, and policy documents that may not appear in standard academic databases. Sources for grey literature include government websites, international organisation repositories (such as the World Bank or WHO), and specialised grey literature databases such as OpenGrey (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p><strong>Contacting experts</strong> in the field can also yield references to unpublished or forthcoming studies that would otherwise be invisible. This is particularly valuable in fast-moving fields where the publication lag means that the most current research may not yet be available in databases (Petticrew and Roberts, 2006).</p>

<h2>7.4 Managing Search Results</h2>
<p>A comprehensive search across multiple databases will typically generate hundreds, sometimes thousands, of results. Managing these results manually is impractical and error-prone. <strong>Reference management software</strong>, such as Zotero, Mendeley, or EndNote, is essential for organising and managing your search results. These tools allow you to import references directly from databases, remove duplicates, organise references into folders or collections, add notes and tags, and generate correctly formatted citations and bibliographies (Hart, 2018).</p>
<p>The use of reference management software should begin on the first day of your literature search. Every reference you encounter should be imported into your reference manager immediately. Do not rely on bookmarks, downloaded PDFs scattered across your desktop, or handwritten notes. These informal systems invariably fail as the number of sources grows, leading to lost references, incomplete citations, and unnecessary duplication of effort (Ridley, 2012).</p>
<p>As you import references, develop a consistent system for tagging and categorising them. Tags might reflect the theme of the article, its methodology, its relevance to your research question, or your assessment of its quality. This categorisation will be invaluable when you begin to organise and write your review, as it allows you to quickly retrieve all sources relevant to a particular theme or argument (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>`;
}

function ch08() {
  return `<h1>Chapter 8</h1><h2 style='font-size:2rem;margin-bottom:4px;'>Inclusion and Exclusion Criteria: How to Decide What Goes In</h2><blockquote><strong>Learning Objectives</strong><br>By the end of this chapter, you will be able to:<br>* Explain the purpose and importance of inclusion and exclusion criteria<br>* Develop appropriate criteria based on your research question and scope<br>* Apply criteria consistently during the screening process<br>* Document your selection process transparently<br>* Justify your inclusion and exclusion decisions to examiners and reviewers</blockquote><h2>8.1 Why Criteria Matter</h2>
<p>After conducting your database search, you will have a large pool of potentially relevant references. The next step is to determine which of these references should be included in your review and which should be excluded. This selection process must be governed by explicit, pre-defined criteria. Without clear criteria, the selection process is subjective and potentially biased: the reviewer may unconsciously include studies that support their hypothesis and exclude those that challenge it. Explicit criteria protect against this bias and ensure that the selection process is transparent and reproducible (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>Inclusion and exclusion criteria define the characteristics that a study must have (inclusion) or must not have (exclusion) to be eligible for the review. These criteria are derived from the scope of the review, as defined by the research question and the conceptual, temporal, and geographical boundaries discussed in the previous chapter. The criteria should be specific enough to allow consistent decisions but flexible enough to accommodate the natural variation in how studies are designed and reported (Petticrew and Roberts, 2006).</p>

<h2>8.2 Types of Criteria</h2>
<p>Inclusion and exclusion criteria typically fall into several categories. <strong>Topic or content criteria</strong> specify the subject matter that must be addressed. For example, the study must investigate remote working and employee wellbeing; studies that investigate remote working but not wellbeing are excluded. <strong>Population criteria</strong> specify the participants or subjects of the study. For example, the study must involve employees in the technology sector; studies of university students or healthcare workers are excluded (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p><strong>Methodology criteria</strong> specify the research designs or methods that are acceptable. For example, the review may include only empirical studies, excluding theoretical papers and opinion pieces. Or it may include only studies using validated measurement instruments for wellbeing. <strong>Publication criteria</strong> specify the types of publications that are eligible. For example, the review may include only peer-reviewed journal articles, excluding conference papers, book chapters, and grey literature. <strong>Language criteria</strong> specify the languages in which studies must be published to be included. Most reviews include only English-language publications, but this should be acknowledged as a limitation (Petticrew and Roberts, 2006).</p>
<p><strong>Date criteria</strong> specify the time period covered. For example, the review may include only studies published between 2010 and 2025. This should be justified with reference to the evolution of the field: if the concept of remote working has changed significantly due to the COVID-19 pandemic, studies from before 2020 may be of limited relevance to the current context (Hart, 2018).</p>

<div class="diagram-container"><div class="diagram-header">Common Inclusion and Exclusion Criteria</div><div class="diagram-grid grid-2"><div class="diagram-box"><h4>Typical Inclusion Criteria</h4><p>* Addresses the specific topic/variables<br>* Relevant population/context<br>* Empirical study with primary data<br>* Peer-reviewed journal article<br>* Published in English<br>* Published within date range<br>* Uses validated instruments</p></div><div class="diagram-box" style="border-top-color:var(--gold);"><h4>Typical Exclusion Criteria</h4><p>* Tangentially related topic<br>* Irrelevant population/context<br>* Opinion piece, editorial, commentary<br>* Non-peer-reviewed source<br>* Published in language reviewer cannot read<br>* Published before cut-off date<br>* Conference abstract only (no full text)</p></div></div></div>

<h2>8.3 The Screening Process</h2>
<p>The screening process typically proceeds in two stages. In the first stage, <strong>title and abstract screening</strong>, the reviewer reads the title and abstract of each reference returned by the search and makes an initial decision about whether it is potentially relevant. At this stage, the decision should be inclusive: if there is any possibility that the study is relevant based on the title and abstract, it should be retained for full-text screening. Only clearly irrelevant references should be excluded at this stage (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>In the second stage, <strong>full-text screening</strong>, the reviewer retrieves and reads the full text of each reference that passed the title and abstract screening. Each study is assessed against the full set of inclusion and exclusion criteria. Studies that meet all inclusion criteria and do not meet any exclusion criteria are included in the review. Studies that fail one or more criteria are excluded, and the reason for exclusion is documented (Petticrew and Roberts, 2006).</p>

<h2>8.4 Documenting the Selection Process</h2>
<p>Transparency in the selection process is essential for credibility. For systematic and scoping reviews, the selection process is typically documented using a <strong>PRISMA flow diagram</strong>. This diagram shows the number of records identified through database searching, the number of duplicates removed, the number screened at title and abstract stage, the number excluded at this stage, the number assessed at full-text stage, the number excluded at this stage (with reasons), and the final number of studies included in the review (Moher et al., 2009).</p>
<p>Even for narrative reviews that do not use a formal PRISMA diagram, the reviewer should provide a clear description of how sources were identified and selected. This description, typically presented in the methodology section, should include the databases searched, the search terms used, the time period covered, and the criteria used to determine relevance. The more transparent the process, the more credible the review (Hart, 2018).</p>

<h2>8.5 Handling Borderline Cases</h2>
<p>In practice, not every reference will be clearly inside or outside the scope of your review. Borderline cases are inevitable, and they require careful judgment. When you encounter a borderline case, consider the following questions. Does the study address your research question, even if indirectly? Does it provide important theoretical or methodological context? Would excluding it leave a significant gap in your analysis? If the answer to any of these questions is yes, the study should probably be included (Booth, Sutton, and Papaioannou, 2016).</p>
<p>When in doubt, err on the side of inclusion, particularly in the early stages of your review. You can always decide to exclude a source later if it proves to be tangential. It is much harder to discover that you missed an important source after your review is written and submitted. Keep a record of borderline decisions and the reasoning behind them, as examiners may ask about them (Ridley, 2012).</p>`;
}

